==English==

===Etymology===
From 14th century {{inh|en|enm|gogelen||to roll (eyes), to look sideways}}. Earlier source is unknown. The noun is attested from the 17th century. Compare {{cog|ga|gog||a nod, a slight motion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɡɒ.ɡəl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-goggle.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒɡəl|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[stare]] (at something) with [[wide]] [[eye]]s.
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Offing|chapter=IV, XII, and XV|passage=[...] she frowned a displeased frown and told me for heaven's sake to stop '''goggling''' like a dead halibut. [...]<!--ch. XII--> She left me fogged and groping for the inner meaning, and I could see from Aunt Dahlia's '''goggling''' eyes that the basic idea hadn't got across with her either. <!--ch. XV-->[...] I didn't want to be hampered by an audience. When you're pushing someone into a lake, nothing embarrasses you more than having the front seats filled up with '''goggling''' spectators.}}
#*{{quote-journal|en|December 7 2021|Jesse Hassenger|{{w|Leonardo DiCaprio}} and {{w|Jennifer Lawrence}} cope with disaster in the despairing satire ''{{w|Don't Look Up (2021 film)|Don’t Look Up}}''|url=https://www.avclub.com/leonardo-dicaprio-and-jennifer-lawrence-cope-with-disas-1848171832|work=AV Club|text=the rational pleas of astronomer Dr. Randall Mindy (Leonardo DiCaprio) and grad student Kate Dibiasky (Jennifer Lawrence) for the public to trust science and understand the seriousness of the coming disaster will sound uncomfortably familiar to anyone who has '''goggled''' in disbelief at COVID and/or vaccine denialism.}}
# To [[roll]] the eyes.
#* {{quote-book|en|1744|Samuel Butler|{{w|Hudibras}}||297|Inflam’d all over with Disgrace, / To be seen by her in such a Place; / Which made him hang his Head, and scoul, / And wink, and '''goggle''' like an Owl|origyear=1678|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=GxsUAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA297}}

